# Shaders — GLSL conventions

## Versions & precision
- Write GLSL 1 syntax in drei `shaderMaterial` / three `ShaderMaterial` (`varying`, `gl_FragColor`). On WebGL 2, three@0.185.1 prepends `#version 300 es` and the mapping defines itself; leave `glslVersion` null unless you write `in`/`out` yourself
- Set precision in ONE place: `material.precision = 'mediump'` in the `shaderMaterial` callback (Units 9 and Cover shaders). Do not also put a `precision` line in the GLSL
- Justify `highp` in `docs/shader-note.md` line 4 if you need it
- Wrap clock uniforms (`% period`) so they stay small under `mediump`

## Naming
- Prefix uniforms with `u` — `uTime`, `uMap`, `uHot`
- Prefix varyings with `v` — `vUv`, `vNormal`, `vWorldPos`
- Do not redeclare what three prepends (`position`, `normal`, `uv`, `projectionMatrix`, `modelViewMatrix`, `normalMatrix`); document custom attributes in the material file

## Files & strings
- Template literals in `.js`/`.jsx` with a `/* glsl */` tag
- One material per file (`SleeveMaterial.jsx`), uniform block documented at the top

## Colour
- A sleeve picture is tagged `SRGBColorSpace` before upload, and the fragment shader keeps `#include <colorspace_fragment>`. Missing either one compiles and looks wrong

## Comments (required)
Every custom shader file must include at the top:
1. **Purpose** (one line)
2. **Inputs** (uniforms/textures)
3. **Coordinate spaces** (object/world/view/uv)
4. **Performance notes** (loops, texture fetches, discard usage)

## Safety
- Avoid `discard` in depth-writing passes unless the reason is written down
- Watch `normalize` on collapsed vectors — guard with epsilon when interpolating
- Look the sleeve picture up at `vUv`, not `gl_FragCoord` or view direction, or it swims as the camera orbits

## Reviews
- Any new `for` loop or `discard` in GLSL needs a sentence in `docs/shader-note.md` and a reviewer's sign-off in the PR
- Pasting Unit 9's bulb onto the sleeve is not an authored effect
